edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065335 #edb/ref 1065 1275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065335 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40623a7d-e08a-5ec3-a3d6-1fac6307b20a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065335 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535347 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535354 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535355 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186092217 